**Redesign the post-Send result panel in APILens. The goal is to make the three-layer analysis pipeline visible and primary, with the HTTP response as a subordinate output.**

**Current behaviour (replace this):**
After Send, the UI shows a status code, response time, and a JSON body viewer. Analysis results (Layer 1 warnings, Layer 2 heuristics, Layer 3 AI) appear as incidental cards above or around the response. The response feels like the main event.

**New behaviour (implement this):**

The entire area below the request builder should become a "Pipeline Result" panel. It has two sections, always rendered in this order: Analysis Pipeline (top), then HTTP Response (bottom). A thin labelled divider separates them.

**Section 1 — Analysis Pipeline**

Three layer cards always render after every request, regardless of whether the request succeeded, failed, or was blocked. They are never hidden. Each card has the same anatomy:

Each card has a header row containing: a layer number pill ("L1", "L2", "L3"), a layer name ("Validation", "Heuristics", "AI Triage"), an icon (shield for L1, radar for L2, sparkles for L3), a status badge on the far right, and a processing time in small muted text (e.g. "~0ms", "~1ms", "~3.2s").

The status badge has four states: "Pass" (green) when the layer ran and found nothing, "Warning" (amber) when the layer found something non-blocking, "Blocked" (red) when the layer stopped the request, "Skipped" (gray) when the layer was not applicable (e.g. Layer 2 skipped on first request with no baseline data, Layer 3 skipped because status was 200 and user didn't click Explain).

The card body shows what the layer actually did — not just what it found, but what it checked. This is the key difference from the current design.

Layer 1 body should always list the rules it ran, even when they all passed. Format: a checklist with pass/fail icons. Rules: schema validation, required headers present, SSRF protection check, content-type consistency, JSON body validity. Each rule shows its name and either a green checkmark or a red/amber flag with the specific finding. When a rule fails, show the rule name as a code pill (e.g. `MISSING_CONTENT_TYPE`), the human explanation, and a suggested fix inline.

Layer 2 body should show the session context it used: number of previous requests to this endpoint, rolling average latency, current streak of same status codes. Format: a small data table — "Endpoint baseline: 10 requests, avg 98ms", "This request: 241ms (+2.4× avg)", "Auth streak: 1 consecutive 200 (reset)". When a heuristic fires, highlight that row in amber. When there's no baseline yet, show "Insufficient data — 5 requests needed to establish baseline" in muted text, not an empty card.

Layer 3 body has two states. Default (no explanation requested): show a single muted line — "No issues detected — explanation not triggered. Click Explain to analyse this response." with an Explain button. Active (after Explain clicked or auto-triggered on 4xx/5xx): show the full AI explanation with the response meaning, root cause if applicable, and concrete fix. The explanation should be structured: a "What happened" paragraph, a "Why" paragraph, and a "Fix" section with code-formatted suggestions. Show "from cache" or "fresh · 3.1s" and a "Save to team knowledge" button in the card footer.

**Section 2 — HTTP Response**

Below a divider labelled "Response", show the existing response viewer: status code (large, colour-coded), response time, a "Why" one-line summary strip, then Body and Headers tabs with the JSON viewer. The status code area should gain a contextual latency badge ("fast", "normal", "slow +2.4×") pulled from Layer 2 data.

**Layout and visual treatment:**

The three layer cards should use a consistent left border colour to identify their layer: red/pink for L1 (security/validation), amber for L2 (heuristics), purple for L3 (AI). This makes the three layers scannable at a glance even when collapsed. Cards should be collapsible — clicking the header toggles the body. Default state: all expanded. After the user has used the app a few times, they should be able to collapse layers they trust.

Add a "Pipeline summary" strip at the very top of the result panel, before the three cards. One horizontal row showing: L1 status dot → L2 status dot → L3 status dot, with a single summary sentence: "All checks passed · 241ms · no issues" or "L1 blocked · request never sent" or "L2 flagged · L3 explained". This is the TL;DR that a developer reads in one second.

**What this achieves:**
The app no longer looks like Postman with extra warnings. It looks like a debugging pipeline where the developer can see exactly what each layer checked, what it found, and why it made its decision. The response is the outcome of that pipeline — not the product.

